    \"<span class=sf-dump-key>avg_distance_to_sun_km</span>\" => \"<span class=sf-dump-str title=\"29 characters\">Approximately 57.9 million km</span>\"
    \"<span class=sf-dump-key>brief_intro_composition</span>\" => \"<span class=sf-dump-str title=\"665 characters\">Mercury is the smallest planet in our Solar System and the closest to the Sun. Its highly eccentric orbit takes it very close to the Sun (perihelion) and then much farther away (aphelion). The surface of Mercury, heavily pockmarked with craters resembling our Moon&#039;s, is a testament to billions of years of impacts. Due to its thin atmosphere, there&#039;s a dramatic temperature swing, with daytime temperatures soaring to 430&#176;C (800&#176;F) and nighttime temperatures plummeting to -180&#176;C (-290&#176;F). Its primary composition includes a large iron-nickel core, a silicate mantle and crust, and an extremely thin exosphere formed by atoms ejected from its surface by solar wind</span>\"
  </samp>]
